1. Definitions.
A. As used in this chapter, the term:
(1) Child means any person under 17 years of age.
(2) Loiter means to sit, stand, or otherwise remain or linger idly at, on or near a public place.
(3) Parent means the natural parent or parents, guardian, or other person having the legal care, custody, or control of a child.
(4) Public place means any public road, street, or highway or any private street or other area for the passage of automobiles which is open to the public; public or private areas open to the public which are used or intended to be used for the parking of automobiles; the grounds of public school buildings; public parks, playgrounds, athletic fields, or other public recreational areas; or other public or private places located outdoors which are open to the public.
B. No parent shall knowingly allow a child for whom the parent is responsible to loiter between the hours of 12:00 midnight and 5:00 a.m. Nothing contained in this section shall be construed to apply to any child who is:
(1) On an errand at the direction of the child's parent or guardian, without any detour or stop;
(2) In a motor vehicle involved in interstate travel;
(3) Engaged in an employment activity, or going to or returning home from an employment activity, without any detour or stop;
(4) On the sidewalk abutting the child's residence or abutting the residence of a next-door neighbor if the neighbor did not complain to the police department about the child's presence;
(5) Attending an official school, religious, or other recreational activity supervised by adults and sponsored by the town, county, school district, a civic organization, or another similar entity that takes responsibility for the child, or going to or returning home from, without any detour or stop, an official school, religious, or other recreational activity supervised by adults and sponsored by the town, county, school district, a civic organization, or another similar entity that takes responsibility for the minor;
(6) Exercising First Amendment rights protected by the United States Constitution, such as the free exercise of religion, freedom of speech, and the right of assembly; or
(7) Married or had been married or had disabilities of minority removed in accordance with law.
(8) Involved in an emergency. As used herein the term "emergency" means an unforeseen combination of circumstances or the resulting state that calls for immediate action. The term includes, but is not limited to, a fire, a natural disaster, or automobile accident, or any situation requiring immediate action to prevent serious bodily injury or loss of life;
C. Any person convicted of violating the provisions of subsection (B) of this section shall be punished as follows:
(1) First offense: A fine of not more than $100.00; and
(2) Second or subsequent offense: A fine of not more than $300.00.
D. In lieu of assessing a fine under subsection (C) of this section, the court may require the person to undergo counseling designed to inform such person of the importance of a proper home environment and close supervision of minor children. (Council, 7/13/1988)

